#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22d9bb51-7d28-5203-a100-671bfb7b1984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281995} {:answer/id #uuid "a39c00a4-afcb-520f-b854-4bc1d26a2c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4$ and $2\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283115} {:answer/id #uuid "b2234da2-6880-5948-bede-cdef269e6b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$8$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283232} {:answer/id #uuid "cbccc52e-55e7-5e83-85bf-7ec1fde391a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283470} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3590a4-fe8c-559b-a199-73736d3c5178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283921}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283115} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a67a626-3555-5355-acc5-d65f47afb3ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fc47bd40-4608-5f54-a026-2eadf6dc5b59" :question/math-academy-id "q-16452" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=4$ and ${b}^{2}=2.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=4,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{4}=2.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=2,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=2,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2=4,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=2\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ea4dfa-3746-512d-a4e5-fa19281f7cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281707} {:answer/id #uuid "119639f4-439c-5028-9a09-08e66ef5732b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281856} {:answer/id #uuid "376b6ab6-79ed-53f0-aaa3-87bd75aa6c79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$12$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282182} {:answer/id #uuid "66196dd8-4f71-57e1-8517-24340f5f07aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{3}$ and $2\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282569} {:answer/id #uuid "95c0623c-a78b-5fec-8c1e-678b85527dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282981}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282569} :answer-field/id #uuid "29260e1c-0c08-5549-8699-d160c488888a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2a341dec-953c-5c37-b7a1-a5cd95c6de7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-16454" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{12}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=3$ and ${b}^{2}=12.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=3,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=12,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{3},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}=4\\sqrt{3},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=2\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bb72661-686b-55b7-acd2-2a3bd4990839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\cdot {2}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284881} {:answer/id #uuid "0fa45466-3a87-5294-bb09-177ec594bc1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284925} {:answer/id #uuid "7da55036-6972-52f3-847b-a31f95df7852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285944} {:answer/id #uuid "968393cd-2595-5198-a30d-859a805cd56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286156} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d61fb9-24f0-590c-861d-b0dc09f76a1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286998}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286156} :answer-field/id #uuid "05333763-722c-5f4d-b56e-21e02dec7970" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "15807573-b299-5e98-85b6-db47d2fbe91c" :question/math-academy-id "q-1952" :question/problem "Given that $y={2}^{9x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={2}^{u},\\,u=9x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=9.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{u} \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{9x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290437 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39f1030c-14e2-59e9-a189-ae72636d7cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}+\\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288429} {:answer/id #uuid "831fe1aa-d1a9-59de-914e-66c8e45135cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{e}^{x}+{2}^{x}ln⁡2+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288914} {:answer/id #uuid "a92a1f84-319a-5ffc-912d-da344601d75d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}}{5}+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289153} {:answer/id #uuid "bfec5a66-eb21-510f-ac20-724d2da23f30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{e}^{x}+\\frac{x}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289301} {:answer/id #uuid "f611671a-7317-5307-aefb-d9046cadfb32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{e}^{x}+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289655}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289655} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b01bf45-11e5-550e-91d5-6d78dcabad9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e3b4a92d-a353-507a-becb-bee354a44152" :question/math-academy-id "q-2277" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (5{e}^{x}+{2}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (5{e}^{x}+{2}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{e}^{x}+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25701e43-e3df-5d1e-8ac3-09bcc9dcf7bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{9}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}+3{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288280} {:answer/id #uuid "5a21abb6-314e-594e-906a-fc211f164a2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}{{9}^{x}}+{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288662} {:answer/id #uuid "ce26c9e7-48dc-50a0-bce8-7f40380eb504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{9}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}-3{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289408} {:answer/id #uuid "d1df1022-beed-5ec0-bcb0-27462f2a142b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}+{e}^{3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289424} {:answer/id #uuid "d501df4f-3f69-522d-8918-2f3e4ab83e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}-3{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289447}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288280} :answer-field/id #uuid "4104beee-ecab-54db-ad41-31088e6669ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "58d4516f-1cdf-5b1c-9320-38f5fe340903" :question/math-academy-id "q-2281" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({9}^{x}+3{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int ({9}^{x}+3{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {9}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{9}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}+3{e}^{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b75bdb8-3ea8-5b60-874a-dc7e2ed8e8e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\text{ arcsec }y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288555} {:answer/id #uuid "549137e8-c93b-553a-88ae-c56c3509772f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288616} {:answer/id #uuid "6bb704be-6a28-52b3-bd08-a672eef1a367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\text{ arccsc }y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288794} {:answer/id #uuid "7940eb84-6a2b-5ba0-b37c-140d22758e0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\text{ arcsec }y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288866} {:answer/id #uuid "fb429378-2e91-5817-9c2b-96b8e5ed15cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\text{ arccot }y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289688}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288555} :answer-field/id #uuid "775e17c2-e49b-551c-8bb1-e9bbc23ea701" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0be79b52-86d6-59fc-99d7-9763abd0657c" :question/math-academy-id "q-2501" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2}{|y|\\sqrt{{y}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}y=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|y|\\sqrt{{y}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}y=\\text{arcsec}\\,y.\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{|y|\\sqrt{{y}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\int \\frac{1}{|y|\\sqrt{{y}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\text{ arcsec }y+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92fade62-72f5-5c55-aa78-c2d0dbaf2b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293509}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293509} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9711613-beb2-54c9-ab19-cae6c7783280" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5ee547bf-f7e1-51f3-bed2-e58fc1c418f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-277921" :question/problem "![](images/ab/ab3d1fefcefa12133ec4ea7e3e6ce744c88f5a9b25029c950254706097cb4c4d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=7.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "177cbba2-b79a-5f5d-9c48-f99cc18456dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293025}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293025} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b444896-e044-5921-b7f7-0da6232a651c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294016} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89cbf232-7d5e-5370-98f0-434b0ca4424c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293468}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293468} :answer-field/id #uuid "643cdb73-3055-524d-9fe7-30a337996809" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5b7e4e8f-cd81-5ee3-ac55-a56d0433ac6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-277923" :question/problem "For the ellipse \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{81}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=64$ and ${b}^{2}=81.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=64,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{64}=8.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=81,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{81}=9.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=9,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=8.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 9=18,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 8=16.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295041 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "001a0d9f-37c7-5af8-8262-d33442935b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292939}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292939} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0c14e4e-9192-5fc5-bf7f-b01bc1b02f87" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "79f5cd4e-9f15-597c-8503-3bd19ab1ee0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-279101" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb9c9b61de232ab6c190a3a324ff3ee2515c61f72a16e2814d77f94a60255501.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=8$ and the vertical radius has length $b=6.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{8}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295042 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0845d678-553d-5f9b-8f0c-cc3b9b0a3bcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292966}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292966} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e7e8acb-7222-57bd-8d98-c0f67679b115" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "40ba0575-d790-5658-a955-78c24f417888" :question/math-academy-id "q-279125" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e1bfe4335b41f14dce0f3d614ca108a4d6d83c4aa65fc44f169431175c82f1b5.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=8$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{9}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{8}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{81}{4}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{64}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{81} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dea75317-4676-5892-9874-67ea7ff4cdf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{121}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293799}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293799} :answer-field/id #uuid "b698fac5-48ad-5a4b-97a3-0c37682df74c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "087f317c-361a-5e32-80c0-0140a69715d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-279126" :question/problem "![](images/41/410a33acd13bc05e65a8f11defb9828a68bf5b80826305adefe2d008afa245c4.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{11}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{11}{2}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{121}{4}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{121}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295044 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb563e16-bedd-5fd3-b345-0e1ce12a034a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{289}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293850}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293850} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a79c72b-8065-5c15-9144-a0efaabaec31" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eb9dc1b1-fc7f-54d8-8fe7-138d381a4ada" :question/math-academy-id "q-279130" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1fcd12762c7b86e1d40813a747b75e1f9b21d83787824f5f48725ac9a1dddff6.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{17}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{7}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{17}{2}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{289}{4}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{49}{4}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{289}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{49} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295045 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e63fb47-9980-5e87-8d24-ce7eca9acd5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293392}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293392} :answer-field/id #uuid "48bfec57-cd37-5bd5-90c5-e5ddcac17dea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "456e704c-c6f8-5c90-abc7-50e898dff618" :question/math-academy-id "q-279151" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f3972825271a2f9471657919ed3669c63e03cd3740a436ab14cc30ed65b73bc.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{7}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{49}{4}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295046 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b57ce2a3-4e47-5f3d-b3c5-b2a4d2ffbc87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{169}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293641}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293641} :answer-field/id #uuid "6260a0b3-57b4-59ba-b250-89d695b2465a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "09a0ffab-31a1-59c6-932b-05d507e393c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-279158" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c583eabd1719e94dc727b876b944c839a39124adb341d88357cf954dbed79646.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{9}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{13}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{13}{2}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{81}{4}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{169}{4}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{169} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295047 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dc25ece-e5e8-5341-b369-60c89d7b7cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293391}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293391} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b5ea0c3-f3db-587a-a55c-d246902036fa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294196} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8edd52d8-a856-5699-9135-823330f651e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293486}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293486} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2a02e34-a1c4-5302-b6ca-ab014cebd574" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "26bde4a4-e1ca-5778-ae0f-4fa44e3f19d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-279165" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=9$ and ${b}^{2}=4.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=9,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=4,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{4}=2.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=3,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=2.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 3=6,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c243753-ebfb-5bd5-b708-979771972883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293476}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293476} :answer-field/id #uuid "2813c84a-d103-5cb1-bd59-405c9d238f89" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294058} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e23f6045-b753-590b-95e0-5f04f82ba4e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293819}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293819} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f5d06e6-62e7-51fd-886c-846c8398c0d0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "05eafcf1-2e1f-53c8-892c-751fcabef6c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-279166" :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=36$ and ${b}^{2}=16.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=36,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{36}=6.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=16,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=6,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=4.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 6=12,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 4=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295049 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee64a24f-4e3c-5796-94b8-769faa4d203e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293859}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293859} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d433ad7-9ca0-5240-8e86-ff3b484b7323" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294199} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1efda85-532c-5456-9085-146b731f4efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293689}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293689} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb9daf9a-554c-59f2-8703-ec452d9cbcc0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c976c0d3-2975-5e8a-a067-1b42fba6644e" :question/math-academy-id "q-279169" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=4$ and ${b}^{2}=25.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=4,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{4}=2.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=25,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=5,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=2.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 5=10,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295050 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd07ce69-7233-50e2-ade0-35bd41ecc948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293668}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293668} :answer-field/id #uuid "3506445f-e25d-5eed-9620-502f091e6d1e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294113} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e04b67f5-a816-54c3-8155-668351856ed0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293808}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293808} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c5007b9-26b4-5a76-ac1d-02ea913e3344" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f4805803-ce68-53d4-945c-e98dec710bca" :question/math-academy-id "q-279172" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=36$ and ${b}^{2}=5.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=36,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{36}=6.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=5,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=6,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 6=12,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{5}=2\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295051 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "846d7b07-a5c9-50d0-a790-8ca46da7e3fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293457}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293457} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a16efb0-a185-590d-9e35-93c8dcb0f26d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294302} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c1eabad-e452-564a-88e1-27687aa4dde5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293548}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293548} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e9f79fd-01b1-5890-b694-2f2e824a01b1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "21716470-c94c-551d-bba8-b61853f57b3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-279174" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=12$ and ${b}^{2}=9.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=12,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=9,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=2\\sqrt{3},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=3.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}=4\\sqrt{3},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 3=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295052 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92e55227-bf1c-5ea6-85e1-7b51ef62ef62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293508}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293508} :answer-field/id #uuid "440e9a61-755c-5fee-8379-dccea5769774" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294171} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1196f9d-7e68-5b43-b2c7-5648f5cf9901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293622}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293622} :answer-field/id #uuid "88343c54-e5b4-5c7e-8b0e-7c3041c31c26" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d82ddc72-1ed9-51d4-9209-e788e94b9b5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-279197" :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=12$ and ${b}^{2}=49.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=12,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=49,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=7,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 7=14,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}=4\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297339 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bbc6124-c201-533d-8a89-7b77ca50d995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20{x}^{4}{e}^{4{x}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295698}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295698} :answer-field/id #uuid "93a5b787-ef54-5338-bc44-45f2d399376a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "487ee789-7ad7-529c-b59f-58c0bcaef3bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-312451" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={e}^{4{x}^{5}},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u=4{x}^{5}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=20{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (20{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =20{x}^{4}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =20{x}^{4}{e}^{4{x}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297340 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f743bf76-aa68-58e9-907f-2483aa71ceaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296581}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296581} :answer-field/id #uuid "47b334c0-adcd-5c15-b3b3-c3ba23b7d937" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "96fff31f-332c-5572-b655-bad437de2f44" :question/math-academy-id "q-312462" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={5}^{{x}^{3}+1},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,{5}^{{x}^{3}+1}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={5}^{u},\\,u={x}^{3}+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={5}^{u}ln⁡(5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={5}^{u}ln⁡(5)\\cdot (3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}ln⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{u} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}ln⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297341 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38bd8b8c-b4c6-5ce9-a1d8-1966ee4c81bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{ln⁡x}}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295587} {:answer/id #uuid "588dfff7-5cbe-5038-b14d-3d8e751e698d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{ln⁡x}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295779} {:answer/id #uuid "986402eb-7839-5d62-b792-c55aac95a275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296083} {:answer/id #uuid "9902409f-61ad-5f6d-bef9-23a2badd42e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{2x}}{ln⁡3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296087} {:answer/id #uuid "ba798157-3c82-5684-a8b2-fe1f9b64430c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{ln⁡x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296245}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296083} :answer-field/id #uuid "11c70d75-1a42-5da1-9557-cc4e7e234b2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a909a720-f9c7-52e5-a8a4-abd3492e6b5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-31249" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 3\\cdot {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constant $3.$ Then, we integrate the exponential function and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3\\cdot {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ddda862-700e-5259-8a9b-324825fe885d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{x}+\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295436} {:answer/id #uuid "3b3aafd2-10f8-5647-aeef-217f0b123766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295606} {:answer/id #uuid "4aaae1a6-c031-5bc5-a104-23401003f961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295691} {:answer/id #uuid "531024e5-c103-583c-92eb-e4200ac7a6df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{3x}-\\frac{{3}^{2x}}{ln⁡x}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295744} {:answer/id #uuid "56637843-7c20-5243-80d5-7c1405ee9de3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-3{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295769}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295606} :answer-field/id #uuid "32505d16-6c49-5e59-9ed0-fdcb1b385a52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "42f6b48b-f5fd-5245-b823-ea76a025e943" :question/math-academy-id "q-31252" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2{e}^{x}-{3}^{x}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{e}^{x}-{3}^{x}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49d14480-6ea8-53a7-8d9d-e4372b3c13d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡|x|}-{e}^{2x}+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295687} {:answer/id #uuid "4b209e11-101c-567a-969b-9f779fc2d1b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\frac{{e}^{5x}}{ln⁡5}-{e}^{x}+2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295694} {:answer/id #uuid "a3afa67c-3743-529b-80a1-f481ffdb27cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{5}^{3x}}{ln⁡x}+2{e}^{2x}+{x}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296132} {:answer/id #uuid "b65d0920-ff23-58e4-bde5-060a3ca311cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{ln⁡5}+2{e}^{x}+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296224} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd1d280-28cc-5226-a0a1-cc64b4710f4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡5}-2{e}^{x}+2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296612}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296612} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2edd6af-13fe-51fe-9021-b17c8fe0c1c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "73bc3d8b-49e8-5e01-8096-212f791e5c3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-31254" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int ({5}^{x}-2{e}^{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({5}^{x}-2{e}^{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡5}-2{e}^{x}+2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297347 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "585614dd-9ea9-5784-93b4-6914d0f259e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{e}^{x}+\\frac{{4}^{x}}{ln⁡4}+ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295776} {:answer/id #uuid "81771957-0036-5c33-bdc4-df04751e4fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}}+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡4}+3ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295963} {:answer/id #uuid "8598c747-3949-5f9e-8cb4-3d148fb0cdb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}+\\frac{x}{ln⁡4}+3ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295982} {:answer/id #uuid "aed107ef-e155-5c3a-86cd-e0c4ddc3690a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}+\\frac{{4}^{x}}{ln⁡|x|}+4ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296189} {:answer/id #uuid "b9377cb5-4f12-5d35-bfa2-ab4190b617d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{x}+\\frac{{4}^{4x}}{ln⁡2}+2ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296240}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295776} :answer-field/id #uuid "33473edb-2246-524d-982e-ed6605f64e5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8203576d-9480-5aa7-b8ae-908a8c1708f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-31256" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (-2{e}^{x}+{4}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (-2{e}^{x}+{4}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =-2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {4}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}+\\frac{{4}^{x}}{ln⁡4}+ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297358 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03eaae83-aa97-5d62-99ec-86500aa38887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\cdot \\frac{x}{ln⁡5}+{e}^{x}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295284} {:answer/id #uuid "19e3e8af-7108-5d2e-854f-5d3554910a1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡5}+{e}^{x}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295420} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef143b8-e3a9-5d16-af8b-ddc53392af90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡|x|}-2{e}^{x}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296028} {:answer/id #uuid "b91478a3-d7d5-51a3-b0f5-b450152d8c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡5}-{e}^{5x}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296238} {:answer/id #uuid "be5397f5-2ec4-53e9-a9c8-ea1bdd02f904" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡5}-{e}^{x}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296271}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296271} :answer-field/id #uuid "561f5968-33ed-5046-bbef-83726bbb8b1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9412645b-c765-5ef4-bf32-1eae5303c300" :question/math-academy-id "q-31260" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2\\cdot {5}^{x}-{e}^{x}+{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2\\cdot {5}^{x}-{e}^{x}+{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡5}-{e}^{x}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb26afef-455b-5297-b622-9e232fa102cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x+7)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296332} {:answer/id #uuid "80839a19-547c-4ca2-94bb-993fa3f9a75b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577458} {:answer/id #uuid "bdcd2965-c659-42b4-96df-25a652585597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\left(2x\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577459} {:answer/id #uuid "886e5238-5128-4041-b4c9-6957e06baf70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\left(2x+7\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577460} {:answer/id #uuid "cd0bf0d4-06da-4ccd-9691-c576056d141e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\left(2x+7\\right)^{-}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577461} {:answer/id #uuid "cab10951-ff4e-46a1-9533-9c912f129e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\left(2x+7\\right)^{-\\frac{3}{\\placeholder{}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577462} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe2d8e8-70df-4e73-95b1-9c1880cd800a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\left(2x+7\\right)^{-\\frac32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577463}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296332} :answer-field/id #uuid "69dfefe3-2eb8-5512-ab7d-eb2788925109" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2300bd8f-e3d1-544c-8ad1-127cb2f0bedc" :question/math-academy-id "q-314194" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+7}}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x+7.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x+7{)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305068 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19d18b9f-e255-57ca-ab7c-88b4c2c79dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302627} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6a42c8-1bdc-503e-9570-0da4f56a856f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302954} {:answer/id #uuid "b3285bea-a204-504b-a8a8-83953d25e492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303568} {:answer/id #uuid "b32dce3f-9b5f-5183-8ff1-557eecebd978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303569} {:answer/id #uuid "d34457fa-06d8-58fa-b42d-d095408535da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303766}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303766} :answer-field/id #uuid "6987c77e-1216-570b-8517-07ef8d619dd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bbb7c5a8-8019-5513-97b1-79df2dce9bf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-332607" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above and its trajectory passing through the point $(-1,2)$ in the phase space of the system. What is the tangent vector to the trajectory at this point?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nIn our case, the trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(-1,2)$ in the phase space. Substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =-3x+y \\\\  & =-3(-1)+2 \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2x+4y \\\\  & =2(-1)+4(2) \\\\  & =-2+8 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{′}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "081b9976-b738-5ad8-8246-0ca3d055b073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302510} {:answer/id #uuid "30b98092-98d5-590f-9de7-afcb1dea0951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302761} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cb4e39-c649-5e10-9265-aedfa518b502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303752} {:answer/id #uuid "de32daaa-a35e-5748-a3f9-987608c72211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303848} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b1ff99-b754-5f70-aa36-6c945ecc4b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303897}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186303848} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cd7d3d4-3fdc-529c-beff-1989a6bed4e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d224fa0d-c666-58fc-b9ce-f9b761da417b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332612" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above and its trajectory passing through the point $(-1,-2)$ in the phase space of the system. What is the tangent vector to the trajectory at this point?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nIn our case, the trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(-1,-2)$ in the phase space. Substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =2x+y \\\\  & =2(-1)+(-2) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-x+3y \\\\  & =-(-1)+3(-2) \\\\  & =1-6 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{′}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}]}
